00:05:04.462 --> 00:05:05.420
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:08.450
Well, OK, so at a high
level, what is VirusTotal?

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:11.000
EMI MARTÍNEZ: Yeah, so most
people think that VirusTotal is

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:15.150
simply a publicly facing website
to which you can upload a file

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:19.447
and get the [INAUDIBLE] for more
than 70 anti-virus solutions

00:05:19.447 --> 00:05:21.780
in order to get a second
opinion about the maliciousness

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:22.940
of a file.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.950
And that's the
basic understanding

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:26.700
for the novel users.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:29.180
If you are a bit more
curious, you probably

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:33.080
have discovered that we not
only scan files but also URLs,

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.150
domains, IP addresses.

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:38.150
And the bottom line is
that we're receiving

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.430
about two million files a day.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:46.460
And we're doing tons of URL
scanning and domains and IPs.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:49.790
So eventually, what
happens is we're not only

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:51.390
running AVs on files.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:53.120
We are also running
all sorts of tools

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:56.320
that extract metadata and
relationships about files

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.160
and the other
items that we scan.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.170
So for instance, when
you upload a file,

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:01.700
and it happens to
be an executable,

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:04.910
we will run it in a
virtualized environment.

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:07.280
And we'll get to see
what that file does

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:09.200
when it infects a machine.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.420
We will then run all
sorts of micro services

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:14.150
that, for instance, if you
upload an executable file

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:18.110
to VirusTotal or try to
send file to VirusTotal

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:19.940
any compressed
bundle, it will try

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:22.580
to decompress it,
and, if there's

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:25.170
any interesting files within
that bundle, re-upload

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:27.950
to VirusTotal and get
the inner files scanned.

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:31.360
So we do tons of microservices
on the files, URLs,

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.710
and the rest of
items that we scan.

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:36.680
And eventually, what
happens with all this data

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:40.760
is it gets into our data
set, which these days is

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:46.080
over two billion files, and the
end product of all this work

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:50.060
is we get to create a service,
which I usually describe as--

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:52.190
Google and Facebook,
you mix them together,

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:54.840
and you apply them
to the malware field.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:56.210
So why Google?

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:59.330
Because we created this thing
called VirusTotal Intelligence,

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:02.090
which is like a search
engine over malware.

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:04.550
And you get to use over
40 modifiers in order

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:07.670
to identify particular
malware that interests you.

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:10.280
So for instance, you can
do stuff like, "Hey, let me

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:12.770
know all of the malware
that reaches out

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:17.600
to Google Drive upon execution,"
or all the malware that

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:21.290
is detected by a given
anti-virus engine with a given

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:24.980
label and happens to be a
PDF that contains JavaScript.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:27.080
So you can combine
more than 40 modifiers

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:30.140
in order to identify particular
families of malware that

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:31.470
interest you.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:33.020
So that's the Google
side of things.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:34.940
Why do you say that it's
similar to Facebook?

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:37.430
It's similar to
Facebook in that,

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:41.030
for each match of
your search results,

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:45.150
you get to see a profile of
every sample in the data set.

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:48.590
So you get to understand
the individual items

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:51.320
of that malware
and the individual

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:54.260
like through Sandboxing and
virtual environment execution

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:55.160
that we do.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:58.520
But you also get to see the
relationships with other items

00:07:58.520 --> 00:07:59.400
in the data set.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:03.110
So has this file been
downloaded from a given URL?

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:06.010
Or is this file reaching
out to a given domain?

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:07.820
And what other files
are reaching out

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:09.150
to those resources?

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:11.420
So it builds in this
relationship notion

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:14.000
that is similar to
the way that Facebook

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:16.380
works but applied to malware.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:19.370
So we built this intelligence
tool over malware

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:24.050
that many security companies,
security teams worldwise use,

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:27.710
and it has its counterpart as
an API with same functionality

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:29.660
but exposed
programmatically so that you

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:32.919
can integrate with your
programs with the core data set

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:34.429
that VirusTotal builds.

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:38.427
And that's like a very broad
overview of what we do.

00:08:38.427 --> 00:08:40.760
MARK MANDEL: Is this something
we're doing in isolation?

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.100
Or are we doing this in
conjunction with other security

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.590
researchers or
anything like that?

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:48.240
EMI MARTÍNEZ: Yeah, so the
thing is, the technology itself,

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:53.000
we build it here in VirusTotal,
but it's used by tons

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:54.500
of researchers worldwide.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:57.730
So the first people that use
it is the anti-virus vendors

00:08:57.730 --> 00:09:00.650
participating in VirusTotal,
which are over 70

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:04.370
of them, including companies
like SimonTech, Kaspersky,

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:06.680
McAfee, et cetera.

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:10.310
And the second set of users
that we'll be using this

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:14.940
is security teams and
big and medium companies.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:17.945
So at the end of the
day, they use the tool

00:09:17.945 --> 00:09:20.480
and generate knowledge
about malware

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:23.690
that then contributes
the world-wide defenses

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:25.400
against the bad guys.

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:28.160
So in some way, we
are an ecosystem,

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:32.990
where all the security-related
individuals co-exist and work

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:36.970
with each other, sharing the
knowledge through VirusTotal.

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:39.530
MELANIE WARRICK: How did
the VirusTotal idea get off

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:40.610
the ground to begin with?

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:43.220
EMI MARTÍNEZ: So it's a
very interesting story.

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:47.000
The founder, Bernardo,
way back early 2000,

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:49.820
he was doing anti-virus
comparatives--

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:55.090
so performance comparatives of
anti-viruses for a magazine.

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:57.080
And he had two needs.

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:01.190
One-- he needed to compare
the anti-virus solutions,

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:03.710
and doing it manually
was difficult.

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:06.530
So he wanted a way to
automate that task.

00:10:06.530 --> 00:10:08.330
And secondly, he
needed samples--

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:12.170
malware samples-- in order
to test this product.

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:16.420
So he hired a team
that went on to build

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:18.190
the very first VirusTotal.

00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:21.850
And since he had this need
and he had already build this,

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:24.470
he said, "Hey, why not
expose it publicly?

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:27.290
Because I'm going to
receive malware samples.

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:29.800
I'm going to give value
to other users that

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:32.180
might have a similar use case."

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:35.710
So these days, we don't promote
at all anti-virus comparatives

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:36.510
with VT.

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:39.150
It's not something
you should be doing.

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:41.380
But that's how it was born.

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:44.470
And thereafter, it's
grown into a tool

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:47.800
that mostly any security
researcher in the world

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:51.280
is using in order to have a
second opinion about files.

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:54.712
And since we are receiving like
two million files per day--

00:10:54.712 --> 00:10:55.420
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:58.840
EMI MARTÍNEZ: --we get to have
a massive data set with all

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:02.800
the behaviors, with all the
related items, and hence,

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:06.850
these days, it's more of a
malware research tool rather

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:09.197
than its initial use case.

00:11:09.197 --> 00:11:10.780
MARK MANDEL: When
people submit stuff,

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:13.150
is this an entirely
automated process?

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:15.720
Do we have security researchers
on our side also doing stuff?

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:17.260
Is it combined?

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:19.280
What's that backhand
process look like?

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:22.330
EMI MARTÍNEZ: OK, so what
happens is either they use

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:25.570
the API or the public
web interface, which,

00:11:25.570 --> 00:11:27.850
under the hood, uses the API.

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:29.980
So you submit a file.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:33.130
This reaches certain Google
Cloud infrastructure, which

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:35.360
happens to be in App Engine.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:39.360
App Engine is serving both
the public-facing front end.

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:42.580
And it's also acting as a
back end for the data store.

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:44.950
When the file
reaches App Engine,

00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:48.850
we copy it to Google
Storage and create a task

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:50.950
to say, "Hey, analyze this."

00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:55.240
And this task goes into pubsub
systems and microservices

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:56.650
that we run our end.

00:11:56.650 --> 00:11:58.930
For instance, one
of them is simply

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:03.520
all of the AVs running
in Google Compute Engine.

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:08.960
So we'll subscribe to this
pubsub topic, get the file,

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:11.590
scan it with all
the anti-viruses,

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:14.200
send back the results
either via pubsub

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:18.000
or an HTTP post
in an internal API

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:20.530
to a pending store
in the data store.

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:24.820
Thereafter, once we have all
this knowledge in the data set,

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:27.370
that's when other
researchers come over,

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:31.630
because either they are checking
hashes and files against VT,

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:33.550
and they happen
comment on those files

00:12:33.550 --> 00:12:35.920
and investigate through
the web interface

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:39.820
or because they have some kind
of automation pulling our feeds

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:41.540
of malware, et cetera.

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:43.080
So that's the way it works.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:45.890
And the end output
of this is that, what

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:49.960
they research with VT, they end
up creating rules to detect it.

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:54.670
And so this, in some sense,
nurtures the entire security

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:55.640
industry.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:59.200
And we're making a dent
on billions of users

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:01.750
through these
security products that

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:05.560
happen to do their research
with VT, all the AVs seen in VT,

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:07.610
security teams and
big companies that

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:09.309
try to secure their
users, et cetera.

00:13:09.309 --> 00:13:10.600
MARK MANDEL: That's super cool.

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:11.620
I'm curious to know--

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:13.491
I mean, just on the
GCP side of things--

00:13:13.491 --> 00:13:14.740
why did you choose App Engine?

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:16.160
Has that been a really
great fit for you?

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:17.180
How's that working?

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:19.600
EMI MARTÍNEZ: So if I
rewind back in time,

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:23.680
before the Google acquisition,
we actually ran our own data

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:25.720
center in our office.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:29.710
And it was super noisy, and
you didn't want to be there.

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:33.760
And what happened is we
had a couple of MySQLs,

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:37.480
and we had a couple of
front end [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:41.030
And we ended up spending more
time managing the systems

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:43.230
and doing administration
rather than coding.

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:44.290
It was horrible.

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:47.530
Every single day, we would have
some kind of MySQL corruption

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:49.460
that would lead to downtime.

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:52.540
So once upon a time,
before the acquisition,

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:55.190
Google came over and
actually offered us a deal

00:13:55.190 --> 00:13:58.900
to go on to App Engine
for free in exchange

00:13:58.900 --> 00:14:01.000
for malware intelligence.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.870
And that's how our initial
relationship got forged.

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:06.550
And we started
building App Engine.

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:11.250
And I wouldn't ever return
to the initial days.

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:14.140
Right now, we can focus
exclusively on coding

00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:17.460
rather than on managing systems.

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:20.410
And for us, it's very simliar,
because it automatically

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:21.070
scales.

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:23.170
We don't have to
worry about scale.

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:25.270
And it's the best
decision we ever made.

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:27.910
And probably, we wouldn't have
been able to grow to the size

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:31.450
that we are today-- two billion
files in the collection--

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:34.240
without the Google
infrastructure.

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:37.260
This accounts for two
petabytes worth of data

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:38.830
in Google Storage.

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:41.570
And that's only the files
they've [INAUDIBLE] themselves.

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:43.360
Then you have to
count all the metadata

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:44.496
that we generate for them.

00:14:44.496 --> 00:14:46.870
MELANIE WARRICK: Is any of
that data available for people

00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:49.270
to do research on outside
who are doing research

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:50.680
into security issues?

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:52.030
EMI MARTÍNEZ: Yeah, absolutely.

00:14:52.030 --> 00:14:53.620
We have several offerings.

00:14:53.620 --> 00:14:55.040
The ones that I
mentioned before,

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:57.550
VirusTotal
Intelligence, our API,

00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:00.100
they are licensed
by third parties.

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:03.620
It's not only internal research
in VirusTotal and Google,

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:06.790
but rather, if you think about
the biggest companies on earth,

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:09.370
they probably are all
VirusTotal customers.

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:13.210
So are all the anti-virus
vendor and security companies

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:14.080
worldwide.

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:18.460
So they basically research
advanced, persistent threats,

00:15:18.460 --> 00:15:20.830
which are like
state-sponsored attacks

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:22.390
against their companies.

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:25.210
Or they try to address
mass malware, which

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:27.960
is targeting their users
and stuff like that.

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:30.790
And this ends up,
as I said before,

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:33.230
in rules to detect malware.

00:15:33.230 --> 00:15:35.950
And we get to impact
billions of users that way

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:40.750
rather than directly having
billions of users as our users.

00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:41.560
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:43.150
You mentioned also
something else interesting.

00:15:43.150 --> 00:15:44.941
In the process of things
getting processed,

00:15:44.941 --> 00:15:47.500
you actually grab the
file on Compute Engine,

00:15:47.500 --> 00:15:50.530
download it, and then
apply the virus checkers

00:15:50.530 --> 00:15:52.270
and various other
automated systems.

00:15:52.270 --> 00:15:54.280
Do you like isolate this file?

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:56.680
I mean, obviously, this
might be a file that

00:15:56.680 --> 00:15:57.950
may contain malware.

00:15:57.950 --> 00:15:59.560
So how do you make
sure that that

00:15:59.560 --> 00:16:02.980
doesn't spread to potentially
other parts of the system?

00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:06.520
EMI MARTÍNEZ: When we run
the AVs against the files,

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:08.410
we don't really execute them.

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:10.990
It's like a static analysis
for the AV [INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:14.200
So in that sense, there
could be room for infection

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:16.360
if the AV was exploited.

00:16:16.360 --> 00:16:19.540
But it's not like we are
actively infecting the machine.

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:21.940
But if we were
exploited, the nice thing

00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:25.270
is that, since we ran
this in Compute Engine,

00:16:25.270 --> 00:16:28.270
we have a snapshot to
which we can revert,

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:30.190
which would be clean.

00:16:30.190 --> 00:16:33.100
So that allows us to
go back really quickly.

00:16:33.100 --> 00:16:36.900
The second thing that
we do that I mentioned

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:40.880
is that we execute malware
in virtual environments.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:45.990
So this we do, for instance,
in stuff like VirtualBox, which

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:47.800
is our virtualization layer.

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:50.310
And thanks to the
fact that recently

00:16:50.310 --> 00:16:53.290
Google Cloud introduced
nested virtualization,

00:16:53.290 --> 00:16:56.170
we're able to run
stuff like VirtualBox

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:58.360
inside Compute Engine also.

00:16:58.360 --> 00:17:00.340
So that's the way
that it's isolated.

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:02.590
MARK MANDEL: I didn't actually
know you could do that,

00:17:02.590 --> 00:17:04.780
and now I need to do that.

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:06.790
I need to run VirtualBox on GCE.

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:08.536
That's actually really cool.

00:17:08.536 --> 00:17:10.660
EMI MARTÍNEZ: We have also
a hybrid infrastructure,

00:17:10.660 --> 00:17:12.940
because it's not something
that you could do before.

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:16.150
But recently, they did this
nested virtualization stuff.

00:17:16.150 --> 00:17:17.109
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:17:17.109 --> 00:17:19.849
What are some of the more
interesting experiences

00:17:19.849 --> 00:17:21.640
you've had with viruses
or more interesting

00:17:21.640 --> 00:17:24.069
viruses you've seen that
you have done things that

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:26.249
were a little out of the norm?

00:17:26.249 --> 00:17:28.540
EMI MARTÍNEZ: I would say
probably the most interesting

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:32.890
thing I can speak of that was
discovered or tracked down

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:37.300
and completely understood
with VirusTotal is Stuxnet.

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:40.550
So if you recall, Stuxnet
has been attributed,

00:17:40.550 --> 00:17:44.230
though not confirmed-- to
be some kind of malware

00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:47.280
that the Israeli and US
government did in order

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:51.820
to slow down the Nuclear
Enrichment Program of Iran.

00:17:51.820 --> 00:17:56.710
So basically, it was malware
targeting the nuclear stations

00:17:56.710 --> 00:18:01.090
in Iran in order to slow down
the centrifuge of uranium,

00:18:01.090 --> 00:18:03.310
in order to enrich uranium.

00:18:03.310 --> 00:18:06.640
So the cool thing about
this is that it was actually

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:12.700
discovered in 2010, and
the actual sample was in VT

00:18:12.700 --> 00:18:15.160
since 2007.

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:17.770
So for three years,
it was in VT,

00:18:17.770 --> 00:18:20.590
and no one really knew about it.

00:18:20.590 --> 00:18:24.190
So when it was
discovered, and I think

00:18:24.190 --> 00:18:26.770
it was people from
SimonTech, they

00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:29.830
were able to build the
entire picture just

00:18:29.830 --> 00:18:32.350
pulling information
from VT, the initial

00:18:32.350 --> 00:18:35.380
dates when it was seen,
any other samples that

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:39.230
were related to that
initial attack, and so on.

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:41.910
So that's probably one of
the most interesting ones.

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:44.890
But there's tons, like
last year or the year

00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:50.320
before that, we had WannaCry
that was all over the press.

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:52.890
And the researcher
that discovered

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:56.950
the kill switch in order to stop
the propagation of that worm

00:18:56.950 --> 00:19:00.520
was using VT for that.

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:02.540
So it's happening all the time.

00:19:02.540 --> 00:19:06.880
Another interesting use case
for VT is banking Trojans.

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:09.580
So you have this
strain of Trojans

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:12.850
that try to target users
of electronic banking

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:17.590
in order to steal credentials
and do automated transactions

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:20.200
of money, like wire
transfers, to the account

00:19:20.200 --> 00:19:23.630
of either our money mule
or the end attacker.

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:27.610
And teams within banks and
other security companies

00:19:27.610 --> 00:19:31.000
study these banking Trojans
all the time with VT in order

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:35.650
to understand, for instance,
where is the stolen data being

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:36.820
sent to--

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:39.850
which is the command and
control for the malware--

00:19:39.850 --> 00:19:43.660
so that they can take
down that command control.

00:19:43.660 --> 00:19:48.010
And all of a sudden, they
might fix tons of infections

00:19:48.010 --> 00:19:51.250
worldwide just by
taking down the network

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:52.720
infrastructure of the Trojan.

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:53.980
MARK MANDEL: There's
something I also

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:55.210
want to highlight, I
think, that's actually

00:19:55.210 --> 00:19:56.020
super interesting.

00:19:56.020 --> 00:19:57.820
You also run
basically what looks

00:19:57.820 --> 00:20:00.550
like a research community--
like, the VirusTotal community?

00:20:00.550 --> 00:20:02.590
Can you tell us a little
bit more about that?

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:07.840
EMI MARTÍNEZ: Yeah, so it's over
600,000 researchers that sign

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:10.090
up into VirusTotal.

00:20:10.090 --> 00:20:12.980
There's several reasons
why they sign up.

00:20:12.980 --> 00:20:18.470
One of them is because they want
to vote on the files and URLs

00:20:18.470 --> 00:20:22.250
that they scan with VT in
order to, for instance,

00:20:22.250 --> 00:20:26.130
highlight malicious stuff that's
undetected by the anti-virus

00:20:26.130 --> 00:20:28.320
and security industry today.

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:30.900
So that way they can
share this information

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:33.180
with the actual
malware researchers

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:34.770
doing the detections.

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:36.550
Another reason for
signing up is that they

00:20:36.550 --> 00:20:40.260
get to get an API key to
do some programmatic work

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:42.840
with the VirusTotal data set.

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:46.860
And not only can they vote
on items, like I said,

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:49.560
they can also place
comments, giving you

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:52.860
the entire background on a
given file that they uploaded.

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:56.160
So for instance, on a given
file that they upload,

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:59.280
they might place a comment
that has the actual spam

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:01.830
email from which the file came.

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:05.010
And then there's this really
nice contextual information

00:21:05.010 --> 00:21:07.380
for the anti-virus
and security industry,

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:09.810
because not only they
get to see the bullet,

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:12.750
but they also get to
see what the gun was.

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:15.240
And perhaps, even,
they get to understand

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:18.750
who the guy with the gun was.

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:23.320
So for many, the end goal is
to actually understand and know

00:21:23.320 --> 00:21:24.500
your enemy.

00:21:24.500 --> 00:21:26.560
So the bullet is not enough.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.080
You have to see
the entire picture.

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:30.960
And actually the community
helps in that sense.

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:33.810
MELANIE WARRICK: Where are areas
that you'd like the community

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:38.160
to provide more help or more
support or more insights?

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:41.730
EMI MARTÍNEZ: Right now, we are
actually doing a nice effort.

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:46.200
And we have today the
[INAUDIBLE] of our AV partners.

00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:48.540
And we recently
launched an initiative,

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:51.180
which we call The
Multisandbox Project.

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:54.960
So I mentioned that we have
our own virtual set-up where

00:21:54.960 --> 00:21:56.580
we run samples.

00:21:56.580 --> 00:21:58.170
Right now, we are
collaborating also

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.390
with some partners
and new companies that

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:04.680
want to give us sandboxing
execution behavior

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:09.060
data for the samples that
we have and that they see,

00:22:09.060 --> 00:22:11.910
so that not only
we have the notion

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:14.880
of what a file
does upon execution

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:18.840
from the VT point of view but
also from other points of view.

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:21.950
And this is really important,
because often malware does

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:25.080
some kind of cloaking,
whereby perhaps it

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:29.790
won't execute in Spain, whereas
it will execute in China.

00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:32.340
And so if we have partners
all over the world,

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:34.380
we get to see
different behaviors.

00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:36.650
And we get stealthier
against malware

00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:40.180
that's trying to evade
this Sandbox system.

00:22:40.180 --> 00:22:42.930
So that's something that
I would like to actually

00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:45.030
have more partners working on.

00:22:45.030 --> 00:22:48.030
Right now, we have like, I
think, four different companies

00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:49.890
providing Sandbox reports.

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:52.020
And we are quickly growing this.

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:54.510
But I would like more
information in that sense.

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:56.280
MELANIE WARRICK: How
do they get involved?

00:22:56.280 --> 00:22:59.700
EMI MARTÍNEZ: It actually
started off at something called

00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:05.970
the Anti-Malware Testing
Standards Organization in 2017

00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:08.040
or 2018, I think.

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:10.320
So one of the AV
partners approached us

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:16.530
and said, "Hey, we not only
want to be an AV verdicts

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:18.090
kind of partner of VT.

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:21.150
We would like to also
provide our Sandbox reports.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:21.840
Why?

00:23:21.840 --> 00:23:25.170
Because our
off-the-shelf solution

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:27.630
not only does like
static detection

00:23:27.630 --> 00:23:29.430
but also does
behavioral detection.

00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:34.440
And we want users to know that
we have different layers built

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:35.620
into our solution.

00:23:35.620 --> 00:23:38.730
And if, like, a malware
evades the static detection

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:42.730
layer it might get caught
by a subsequent layer--

00:23:42.730 --> 00:23:45.420
for instance, the
behavior detection one.

00:23:45.420 --> 00:23:48.780
So they approached us
and suggested this.

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:51.700
It made a lot of sense for us.

00:23:51.700 --> 00:23:53.850
So we got them on board.

00:23:53.850 --> 00:23:56.460
And when we did
that, we launched

00:23:56.460 --> 00:23:58.830
this initiative called
the Multisandbox Project

00:23:58.830 --> 00:23:59.790
in our blog.

00:23:59.790 --> 00:24:02.160
We reached out to our
partners to tell them

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:03.770
that we were starting this.

00:24:03.770 --> 00:24:07.140
And thereafter,
VirusTotal is very--

00:24:07.140 --> 00:24:10.340
even if it's not known
by my mom at home--

00:24:10.340 --> 00:24:12.940
it's very known in
the security industry.

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:17.160
So it's just people e-mailing
us and saying, "Hey, we

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:18.540
do have a Sandbox set-up.

