00:26:12.824 --> 00:26:15.240
MARK MANDEL: So, Emi, is there
anything else inside Google

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:16.050
Cloud that you use?

00:26:16.050 --> 00:26:17.730
I mean, you mentioned
App Engine and GCE,

00:26:17.730 --> 00:26:18.720
but you've got lots of data.

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:19.719
What do you do in there?

00:26:19.719 --> 00:26:22.460
EMI MARTÍNEZ: We are probably
the guys that use most GCP

00:26:22.460 --> 00:26:25.410
technologies from the user base.

00:26:25.410 --> 00:26:26.470
I won't bet on that.

00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:30.250
But, for sure, we use a
wide variety of tools.

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:32.250
So for instance,
I mentioned that

00:26:32.250 --> 00:26:36.180
every single compressed file
that gets uploaded to VT we

00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:40.299
decompress, and we upload
the inner contents to VT

00:26:40.299 --> 00:26:42.090
to see if there is
anything malicious there

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:45.370
and build the
parent-child relationship.

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:48.090
We do that with
Kubernetes engine

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:51.900
as a microservice that
subscribes to that pubsub topic

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:53.260
that we mentioned before.

00:26:53.260 --> 00:26:56.420
So many of the
microservices that we run

00:26:56.420 --> 00:26:59.670
are actually containers
in Google Cloud.

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:01.895
Something else we
can do, for instance,

00:27:01.895 --> 00:27:05.250
is we are really heavy
users of BigQuery.

00:27:05.250 --> 00:27:07.200
We do it for two things--

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:09.120
in order to do
analytics over our data

00:27:09.120 --> 00:27:13.600
set, and we plug it into stuff
like Google Cloud Data Studio

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:16.270
in order to do nice
charts and graphs

00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:19.320
and understand how our users
are using the platform.

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:23.400
But also, we even use it for
advanced and malware research

00:27:23.400 --> 00:27:24.630
in use cases.

00:27:24.630 --> 00:27:26.910
So for instance,
BigQuery has this thing

00:27:26.910 --> 00:27:30.510
which allows you to insert
user-defined functions, which

00:27:30.510 --> 00:27:33.090
you write JavaScript,
and it gets

00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:36.840
executed against every single
row that you have in BigQuery.

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:39.030
So in the malware field,
there's this thing

00:27:39.030 --> 00:27:42.510
called ssdeep, which is
like a fuzzy hash that

00:27:42.510 --> 00:27:45.390
allows you to understand
similarity between files.

00:27:45.390 --> 00:27:49.320
So there's no way you can
search for similar files

00:27:49.320 --> 00:27:52.660
over a two-billion collection
with standard tools.

00:27:52.660 --> 00:27:53.160
Why?

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:55.050
Because it means that
you have to compare

00:27:55.050 --> 00:27:58.410
this hash and the computation
against every other item

00:27:58.410 --> 00:27:59.130
in the data set.

00:27:59.130 --> 00:28:01.020
So that's two
billion comparisons,

00:28:01.020 --> 00:28:02.760
and you want quick results.

00:28:02.760 --> 00:28:05.520
So we plug this into BigQuey.

00:28:05.520 --> 00:28:09.450
And within seconds,
we know what are

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:13.175
the files similar to a given
sample that interests you.

00:28:13.175 --> 00:28:14.550
And this is done,
because we have

00:28:14.550 --> 00:28:17.970
edit distance running as
user-defined functions

00:28:17.970 --> 00:28:18.631
in BigQuery.

00:28:18.631 --> 00:28:19.380
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:21.296
EMI MARTÍNEZ: For instance,
since we are users

00:28:21.296 --> 00:28:23.880
of the standard App Engine
environment but also

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:26.610
of the Flex environment,
whenever we would have to run,

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:31.050
for instance, like stuff that
won't be only Python only

00:28:31.050 --> 00:28:32.820
or Golang only.

00:28:32.820 --> 00:28:38.160
So we have containers deployed
in Google App and in Flex.

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:43.210
And Flex and happens to not
have the standard environment

00:28:43.210 --> 00:28:44.100
memcache.

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:46.110
So we are also users
of Memory Store,

00:28:46.110 --> 00:28:49.450
which is a recent thing
that was announced.

00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:52.565
And we are using memcache
with Memory Store in App

00:28:52.565 --> 00:28:54.300
and in Flex.

00:28:54.300 --> 00:28:57.690
We are also users, for
instance, of Google Cloud SQL.

00:28:57.690 --> 00:29:00.720
Our main data set is actually
in the Cloud data store.

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:04.980
But we have minor App
Engine services, which run,

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:08.460
for instance, WordPress
for our learning space,

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:11.140
which is VirusTotal.com/Learn.

00:29:11.140 --> 00:29:13.760
So that's our WordPress
running in Google App Engine

00:29:13.760 --> 00:29:16.960
standard environment
in the PHP environment.

00:29:16.960 --> 00:29:19.020
And that connects an
SQL, which happens

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:22.620
to be a Google Cloud SQL.

00:29:22.620 --> 00:29:25.500
As I said, we're also using
App Engine Flex, not only in

00:29:25.500 --> 00:29:26.710
the standard environment.

00:29:26.710 --> 00:29:30.240
We do so when we
have a need to run

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:35.470
a certain code that's not only
a Python only or Golang only.

00:29:35.470 --> 00:29:37.830
And since App
Engine Flex is a bit

00:29:37.830 --> 00:29:39.960
different to the
standard environment,

00:29:39.960 --> 00:29:43.350
it doesn't have
the memcached API.

00:29:43.350 --> 00:29:45.400
So we needed something
like memcache.

00:29:45.400 --> 00:29:49.020
So what we are using instead of
that is the Cloud Memorystore

00:29:49.020 --> 00:29:51.340
offering that
recently got launched,

00:29:51.340 --> 00:29:53.310
which is built with Redis.

00:29:53.310 --> 00:29:56.490
And that's been really useful,
because before that, either

00:29:56.490 --> 00:29:59.580
we had to call out some
kind of internal API

00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:02.760
that was built in the App
Engine standard environment.

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:04.710
Now we no longer
have to do that.

00:30:04.710 --> 00:30:09.120
We are also power users of
stuff like the IM administration

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:10.080
for our users.

00:30:10.080 --> 00:30:13.930
So we need to make sure that,
for instance, an intern doesn't

00:30:13.930 --> 00:30:18.180
