00:31:35.350 --> 00:31:36.141
MARK MANDEL: It is.

00:31:36.141 --> 00:31:39.990
It's coming from Andy
Sheridan @AndyJSH.

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:42.780
He asks what is the best
practice for multi-tenancy

00:31:42.780 --> 00:31:45.120
in Google Cloud
Spanner, especially

00:31:45.120 --> 00:31:47.040
if customers are
not the same size

00:31:47.040 --> 00:31:48.457
and potentially
have unequal load?

00:31:48.457 --> 00:31:49.415
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:31:49.415 --> 00:31:51.360
And so for this week's
answer, we actually

00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:53.290
have one of our
colleagues, Robert Kubis,

00:31:53.290 --> 00:31:55.422
who is joining us to
basically answer the question.

00:31:55.422 --> 00:31:56.880
MARK MANDEL: Thanks
for joining us,

00:31:56.880 --> 00:31:58.707
Robert, because I have no idea.

00:31:58.707 --> 00:32:00.040
How are you doing today, Robert?

00:32:00.040 --> 00:32:00.480
ROBERT KUBIS: Very good.

00:32:00.480 --> 00:32:01.620
Thank you for having me.

00:32:01.620 --> 00:32:02.940
And hello, everyone.

00:32:02.940 --> 00:32:05.910
So this is actually a
really, really good question.

00:32:05.910 --> 00:32:07.980
And to basically
elaborate a little bit

00:32:07.980 --> 00:32:11.220
on how a spanner works behind
the scenes-- so in Spanner,

00:32:11.220 --> 00:32:14.400
our data is organized
based on the primary key.

00:32:14.400 --> 00:32:16.470
So the primary key
you choose, that's

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:18.210
how we order the
data in Spanner.

00:32:18.210 --> 00:32:21.240
And then we split these
data up in chunks.

00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:23.040
And these chunks'
responsibilities

00:32:23.040 --> 00:32:26.800
goes to the Spanner nodes that
you have in your instance.

00:32:26.800 --> 00:32:28.560
So now, that means,
basically, if you

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:31.180
have like multi-tenant
application

00:32:31.180 --> 00:32:35.700
and you use a custom ID, as
an example, for a primary key,

00:32:35.700 --> 00:32:38.370
then all your data which is
belonging to one customer

00:32:38.370 --> 00:32:39.540
would go to one node.

00:32:39.540 --> 00:32:42.120
And basically this one node
is responsible for that.

00:32:42.120 --> 00:32:44.490
And that can lead to
things like hot spotting.

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:47.490
So all your load-- which is
all the data that is loaded,

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:49.620
all the data that is
read, all the queries that

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:51.150
are going against
this customer--

00:32:51.150 --> 00:32:53.850
basically have to be
handled by one node.

00:32:53.850 --> 00:32:55.440
Now, you don't want
that, especially

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:57.820
in cases where
you have customers

00:32:57.820 --> 00:32:59.920
which are of unequal size.

00:32:59.920 --> 00:33:03.330
So you have some large customers
and a lot of small ones.

00:33:03.330 --> 00:33:05.910
And a good way to addressing
this is somehow you

00:33:05.910 --> 00:33:07.800
have to basically
split up your data

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:09.750
in a way that the
responsibility can

00:33:09.750 --> 00:33:13.530
be given to multiple
nodes in your instance.

00:33:13.530 --> 00:33:16.200
One of the best practices that
a lot of people are doing there

00:33:16.200 --> 00:33:19.240
is basically prefixing
your primary key

00:33:19.240 --> 00:33:22.750
or using a larger primary
key in your tables.

00:33:22.750 --> 00:33:25.290
So a prefix could
be, for example, you

00:33:25.290 --> 00:33:27.840
could use some other
element in your table.

00:33:27.840 --> 00:33:30.210
And you hash that
element, and you take

00:33:30.210 --> 00:33:32.490
that as a prefix in your key.

00:33:32.490 --> 00:33:34.170
And with that, your
data is basically

00:33:34.170 --> 00:33:36.060
distributed among
multiple nodes.

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:39.120
And now, if you have
very large customers,

00:33:39.120 --> 00:33:41.650
it gets distributed between
the available resources,

00:33:41.650 --> 00:33:44.840
and it doesn't
matter if you have

00:33:44.840 --> 00:33:47.279
a couple of large customers
and a couple of small ones.

00:33:47.279 --> 00:33:48.570
MELANIE WARRICK: This is great.

00:33:48.570 --> 00:33:48.890
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

